sec.p:
Applying samtools idxstats, an endogenous based score was used to evaluate the potential of the sample being positive for Y. pestis. The score was calculated with the following formula:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\frac{\left(YPS-\left(YS\right)\right)}{M}\times 1000$$\end{document}YPS-YSM×1000where YPS is the number of reads specifically mapping to Y. pestis; YS is the maximum number of reads mapping specifically to any Yersinia species with the exception of Y. pestis and M is the total number of merged reads in the sample. By using the maximum number of reads mapping to another species of the genus Yersinia, the score takes into account different sources of contamination other than Y. pseudotuberculosis. Two of the four positive samples exhibited a score higher than 0.005, a value which indicates a strong positive candidate (Table S3).
